@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-literals Literals and Wrappers
@brief Literal operators and wrappers for typed operands.

@details
Within test expressions, literals such as `1_i` and `42_f` create strongly
typed operands, whilst wrappers like `mt::to_i{expression}` convert dynamic
values. These mechanisms enable clear, type-safe comparisons, improving
readability and correctness in test cases.

Literal operators, available in the dedicated `literals` namespace, allow
constants to be expressed with explicit types, such as integers and
floating-point values. For dynamic values, wrappers convert operands into
recognised comparison types at run-time.

The integer literals follow the C++ syntax: decimal, hexadecimal (`0x10_i`),
binary (`0b101_i`), and octal (`010_i`, which is 8), with digit separators
(`1'000_i`). A decimal point, an exponent, or a value that does not fit in the
type of the literal (e.g. `300_u8`) is a compilation error.

The floating-point literals (`1.5_d`) and wrappers (`mt::to_d{ 1.5 }`)
compare with a tolerance of one unit of the last decimal written (0.1 for
`1.5`), so that `1.45 == 1.5_d` is true. All six comparisons apply it
consistently: values within the tolerance are equal, and neither less nor
greater. Plain floating-point values compare exactly. To set the tolerance
explicitly, independent of how the value is written, use
`mt::approx (1.5, 0.01)`.

This approach ensures that both compile-time constants and run-time values
can be compared accurately and expressively, supporting robust,
maintainable test code.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check typed operands", [] (auto& t) {
  using namespace micro_test_plus::operators;
  using namespace micro_test_plus::literals;

  t.expect (mt::to_i {answer} == 42_i);
  t.expect (mt::to_f {expression} == 42_f);
});
@endcode
